# Lesson 5, your Exercise 5, completed with East-West cards.
title: Your Exercise 5: a weak hand with long spades
id: L05-05
type: exercise
lesson: 5
concepts: 5.1
student: S
board: 9
dealer: N
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  K8     AQ62   AK94   T74
  E  A52    K93    QT7    Q953
  S  JT9764 54     J32    82
  W  Q3     JT87   865    AKJ6

auction:
  N 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
  E Pass
  S 2S*    = Fewer than 8 points and long spades: let's play here
     key: A weak hand with six spades. 2♠ is to play, and North must pass.
     not Pass: Your hand would give North almost no help in 1NT, but it can take tricks with spades as trumps.
     not 2C: Stayman needs 8 or more points.
     not 4S: 4♠ shows 10 or more points. You have 2.
  W Pass
  N Pass   = North must pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  With 2 points your hand is no help in no-trump, but six spades make it worth
  several tricks with spades as trumps. 2♠ says "let's play here," and North
  must pass. South is declarer, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 5
  remind: What is the contract?

play:
  1   W CA  N C4  E C3  S C2
  2   W HJ  N HQ  E HK  S H4
      note: Dummy tries the queen. East has the king.
  3   E SA  S S4  W S3  N S8*
      S8 key: Keep the king: it can win a trick later.
      S8 not SK: Did you notice the ace is already winning? Your king would be wasted.
  4   E CQ  S C8  W C6  N C7
  5   E S5  S S6  W SQ  N SK
  6   N DA  E D7  S D2  W D5
      note: Cash a top diamond while you're in dummy.
      ok: H
  7   N HA  E H3  S H5  W H7
      note: Cash the ace of hearts too.
      ok: D
  8   N DK  E DT  S D3  W D6
      note: And the other top diamond.
  9   N H6  E H9  S S7  W H8
      note: Declarer ruffs.
  10  S SJ  W D8  N H2  E S2
      note: The last trump is drawn.
  11  S DJ  W HT  N D4  E DQ
      note: Lead your last diamond. The defense gets one more trick whatever you do.
  12  E C9  S S9  W CJ  N CT
  13  S ST  W CK  N D9  E C5
      note: 8 tricks: 2♠ made.

play recap:
  The plan: drive out the trump ace and draw trumps, then cash dummy's top
  cards. Once the opponents' trumps are gone, your long spades are winners.
  That's why dummy keeps the ♠K: it wins a trick later.

  2♠ made exactly. In 1NT, North would have taken only about five tricks,
  because South's long spades can't be reached without trumps. Bidding 2♠
  turned a hopeless 1NT into a making part-score.
